  "departures": [
    {
      "what": "Postgres gets db.t4g.medium’s 2 vCPU and 4 GiB on the runner’s first two CPUs, which the harness and Chromium share",
      "direction": "harsher"
    },
    {
      "what": "web and platform share the screening consumer’s last CPU (their fractional-vCPU quotas inside it)",
      "direction": "harsher"
    },
    {
      "what": "one web container takes every upload where production runs two web tasks",
      "direction": "harsher"
    },
    {
      "what": "a 2 ms round trip to Postgres, more than an in-VPC trip plus Multi-AZ’s synchronous commit",
      "direction": "harsher"
    },
    {
      "what": "gp3’s I/O baseline without its burst",
      "direction": "equal or harsher"
    },
    {
      "what": "Postgres runs on two full CPUs; db.t4g.medium earns 24 CPU credits an hour (a 20% baseline per vCPU) and bursts on its balance, and RDS runs T4g in Unlimited mode, so a drained balance is billed rather than throttled (AWS’s documentation; not verified by the harness)",
      "direction": "gentler"
    },
    {
      "what": "mail is a file in an outbox instead of an SES call of about 0.1 s",
      "direction": "gentler"
    },
    {
      "what": "the platform and screening workers run NODE_ENV=test with MAIL_OUTBOX_DIR (NODE_ENV_READS: providers and required settings only)",
      "direction": "none"
    },
    {
      "what": "the web runs NODE_ENV=production with TP_ALLOW_LOCAL_PROVIDERS=e2e (local identity, keys, billing and model providers)",
      "direction": "none"
    },
    {
      "what": "process runtime: the build outputs as processes in cgroups, against an unlimited shared Postgres with a warm cache and no network delay",
      "direction": "gentler"
    }
  ],
